Recent years have seen significant progress in Natural Language Processing (NLP), largely attributed to deep learning models and extensive annotated data. A key area of focus has been understanding textual semantics and pragmatics through sentence representations and discourse analysis. Specifically, predicting discourse markers (DMs) between sentence pairs has been widely adopted to generate effective sentence representations for classification assignments. However, this method has limitations. First, existing models often fail to capture subtle semantic and pragmatic details within sentences. Second, the quality of the final representations heavily depends on the specific task of predicting the discursive connection, making these representations suboptimal for unseen classification tasks. To overcome these issues, this paper introduces a new framework for classifying Discourse Markers (MCDC), which combines discourse marker classification with downstream classification tasks to enhance representation quality. We implement this approach for three specific duties using the MNTR and MLSD datasets: sentiment analysis, headline prediction, and genre classification. Our experiments show that MCDC significantly surpasses state-of-the-art models across all three tasks. The contribution of this work is threefold: (1) we highlight the shortcomings of current models in generating optimal representations for classification tasks, (2) we propose the MCDC framework, and (3) we demonstrate its efficacy using three
